# VoLum workflow (vibe coding + safety nets)

## Every meaningful iteration (before you stop or hand off)

A wayfinder or scratch-planning session that does not change product code,
tests, or user-facing docs skips this loop.

1. **Run tests** when C++/NAM/plugin logic changed: `NeuralAmpModeler/scripts/run-tests-win.ps1` (Release x64); macOS and sanitizer parity commands are in `AGENTS.md` "Fast Commands".
2. **Build and launch the standalone app** when VoLum **UI** changed (layout, controls, styling) so you can judge the result:
   - `NeuralAmpModeler/scripts/run-app-win.ps1` (Release x64). It opens a sandbox library; `-RealLibrary` only when the owner asks.
3. **Append a changelog line** so you can bisect and remember intent:
   - Prefer `NeuralAmpModeler/installer/changelog.txt` (keep project style: date + short note).
   - One line per user-visible or behavior change; include breaking preset/state format bumps.
4. **Update user docs** when behavior is user-facing:
   - Keep `docs/user-guide.en.md` and `docs/user-guide.de.md` in sync.
   - Refresh stable `docs/user-guide-*.png` screenshots when the visible UI changes.
5. **Commit** in a **single coherent chunk** when possible (easy `git revert`). If the user prefers WIP commits, say so in chat.

## Tests are mandatory, not optional

- **Write tests for every confirmed-working feature or bugfix.** Do not wait for the user to ask.
- **Verify each regression test fails with the fix reverted**, doctests and
  scripted end-to-end scenarios alike. One that passes against the broken code
  is worse than none: it reports coverage that does not exist.
- The rule attached to the code you are editing names the test files that own it.

## Code hygiene is part of the task

- **Refactor proactively** when a file crosses ~500 lines or a class accumulates unrelated responsibilities. Don't wait to be asked.
- New controls go in the owner file for that surface, never into a monolith or an umbrella header.
- Spikes are fine; **delete or fold** dead spike code before you call the iteration done.

## Execution

- **You run** builds/tests in this environment; do not only tell the user what to run.
- After failures: diagnose, fix, retry—do not stop after one error.
- Rebuild the standalone with `run-app-win.ps1` (it stops `VoLum` and `VoLum_x64`). If the linker reports a locked VST3, close the host DAW.
- Worktrees: only through `NeuralAmpModeler/scripts/worktree-win.ps1 -Add <branch>` / `-Remove <path> [-DeleteBranch]`. Plain `git worktree remove --force` follows junctions and empties their targets (it emptied the main checkout on 2026-09-23).
- Merge a verified branch into `dev` with `NeuralAmpModeler/scripts/merge-verified-branch.ps1 -Branch <branch> [-DryRun]`; it refuses without green CI on the branch head.
- Shell is **Windows PowerShell 5.x**: chain commands with `;` plus `if ($LASTEXITCODE -ne 0) { exit $LASTEXITCODE }`. Do not use `&&` / `||` — PowerShell 5.x rejects them as invalid statement separators.
- Never `>` / `*>` into a file that git, clang-format or another native tool will read: Windows PowerShell writes UTF-16. Use `git diff --output=<file>`, `Out-File -Encoding utf8`, or `[IO.File]::WriteAllText`. A UTF-16 patch once applied as empty and silently dropped a fix.
- Multi-line `git commit` messages: write the message to a temp file, then
  `git commit -F <file>`. A bash heredoc is invalid here, and a here-string
  passed to `-m` loses its line breaks and fails with pathspec errors.

## Scope discipline

- Do not add product specs, README essays, or ADRs by default. An ADR is allowed
  only when all three hold: hard to reverse, surprising without context, and the
  result of a real trade-off. Product specs live in `.scratch/<effort>/` and are
  deleted when the effort ships.

## Branching

- `main` is the released branch, `dev` is the integration branch.
- After every release lands on `main`, merge `main` into `dev` and push.
- New feature work branches off the latest `dev` as `feature/<short-topic>`; merge feature back into `dev` once verified.
- Only promote `dev` to `main` as part of a release (see `release-manager` skill / `volum-release-packaging` rule).
